Not an upstream bug: the files_settings backend (files-api) scopes every file
to its owner, so the proxy has to tell it who is calling.

On the provider-only upload, retrieve, content, delete and list calls, add the
key's user_id, team_id and metadata to extra_headers. They are taken from the
sanitized key details that add_user_information_to_llm_headers sends on model
calls (callback credentials removed), with its x-litellm-user_api_key_* names
and encoding. Only these three go: the backend needs nothing else, and the
other details (aliases, email) can hold characters httpx refuses in a header.
They are built from the authenticated key with no request headers, so a caller
cannot set them. They go in after the team credentials and the files_settings
entry are merged, and any header of these names from those or any other source
is dropped. Model-routed and managed-file calls, and providers without a
files_settings entry, are unchanged. add_user_information_to_llm_headers is not
needed and does not change what the backend receives.

Needs temporary/files-settings-routing.patch (the four non-upload calls reach the
backend) and temporary/files-extra-headers.patch (retrieve, list and delete
send extra_headers).

Checks: tests/verify_files_ownership.py

